---
import PartRows021 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows021.svelte";
import PartRows022 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows022.svelte";
import PartRows023 from "../../../components/studies/PartRows023.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "part-rows-2026-11-11");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / A row that says whether the part is sanctioned</h2>
    <aside class="nb" aria-labelledby="nb">
      <h3 id="nb">Nota bene · the real goal, found in 02.3</h3>
      <p>
        The subject is the tool the player uses to choose the part for a slot.
        It is one bespoke thing in the Hangar. It has to do its own job well and
        conform enough to not feel off. It does not have to be a reusable
        pattern.
      </p>
      <p>
        The text below is the question as first asked. It leaves open what the
        row is made of without saying what the row belongs to, and the first
        three rounds answered it as a general list control.
        <a href="#tool">02.4</a>
        starts again from the tool.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <p>
      In <a href="/hangar">the Hangar</a> the player selects a mount, and a list
      shows one row for each part that mount accepts. Pressing a row fits its
      part. A part is either sanctioned for scored play or unsanctioned.
      Unsanctioned parts can be fitted only while Playground is on, when nothing
      is scored. The row has to say what the part is, whether it is fitted, and
      whether it is sanctioned. Today the fitted row is hard to tell from the
      row under the pointer, and an unsanctioned part carries only a small grey
      caption.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hard case is a forbidden part: an unsanctioned part that is still
      fitted when Playground is off. Turning Playground off unfits nothing, so
      the part stays on its mount and blocks launch. The fitted row is lime, the
      colour of a choice made. This row is fitted too, and it has to show that
      the choice must be undone.
    </p>
    <dl>
      <dt>Fixed</dt>
      <dd>
        Which parts a mount accepts, that pressing a row fits its part
        immediately, Playground's rules for launch and scoring, and what lime
        means.
      </dd>
      <dt>Hypothesis</dt>
      <dd>
        With Playground off, the list hides unsanctioned parts. A forbidden part
        stays in the list, and its row says the part must be removed, or
        replaced if the mount cannot be left empty. With Playground on,
        unsanctioned parts are listed and marked. This would change the
        Playground spec, which today shows them as unavailable.
      </dd>
      <dt>Open</dt>
      <dd>
        What the row is made of, how it shows each of the four states, and what
        words it uses for sanctioned and unsanctioned.
      </dd>
      <dt>Outside</dt>
      <dd>
        How the mount on the ship drawing shows the same states. The
        <a href="/lab/studies/ship-slots-2026-11-11">ship-slots study</a>
        covers that, and its answer must match this one.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <aside class="stamp" aria-labelledby="stamp">
      <h3 id="stamp">Question check · 11 November 2026</h3>
      <p>
        An independent reviewer tested this section against the question rubric
        and found no false claim. Of five findings, four are corrected above and
        one was declined. The section was then reworded for readability. The
        current text has not been retested.
        <a href="/lab/reviews/part-rows-2026-11-11">Full result and findings</a
        >.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Criterion</th>
            <th scope="col">Result</th>
            <th scope="col">Reason</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">One subject</th>
            <td>Pass with findings</td>
            <td>
              The study has one subject, the part row in four states. It also
              asks what the list contains and what the classes are called.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Grounded</th>
            <td>Pass with findings</td>
            <td>
              Every fixed item exists as described. The section left out how a
              fitted part is removed and what the Playground spec says today.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Consequence</th>
            <td>Pass</td>
            <td>
              If the study succeeds, a row shows plainly whether its part is
              fitted and whether it is sanctioned, and a forbidden part looks
              blocked.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <section>
    <details id="pane">
      <summary>02.1 / The row as a pane of the console</summary>
      <p>
        The first answer builds the row the way the tabs are built. A row is a
        pane on the console surface with two cut corners and no outline. The
        drawing sits in a small dark window, so its colours hold in every state.
        The name leads, the catalog description follows in small text, and mass
        closes the row. The category letter is gone.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Fitted</strong>
          fills the pane lime, as the selected tab does, and says Fitted. A
          pointer tints the other rows faintly and leaves the fitted row alone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Unsanctioned</strong>
          closes the row with a hatched band and replaces the description with
          “Unsanctioned · no score”. The band takes the row's own quiet colour,
          so it carries onto the lime of a fitted row.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Forbidden</strong>
          loses the fill and becomes a red frame. It keeps Fitted and the hatch,
          names the remedy, and cannot be pressed. The mount is emptied with
          Remove fitting, or another row is pressed.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The four lists follow the hypothesis: with Playground off, unsanctioned
        parts are hidden unless fitted. Rows can be pressed, and in the two
        lower lists the forbidden row leaves once its part is gone. Parts,
        classes, descriptions and drawings are the game's own. Each list keeps
        its own mount and fitting on this page only.
      </p>
      <PartRows021 client:load />
      <p>
        This shows how the rows look and behave in a list. It does not show them
        in the Hangar's column beside the ship, at the Hangar's width, or by
        touch. The words on the row are working words.
      </p>
      <h3>What 02.1 showed</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Kept: the hatched band.</strong>
          Diagonal lines in the padding at the end of the row mark an
          unsanctioned part. It reads as this game's own and it carries onto a
          fitted row. It is the working answer for the unsanctioned mark, not
          yet final.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Stacked panes read as push buttons.</strong>
          The tabs work because they share one line whose two cut ends make it a
          single sliding control. Rows cut one by one and piled up lose that:
          each becomes a button, and a list of parts is not a set of buttons.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The dark window is wrong.</strong>
          A drawing boxed in black inside each row belongs to web games of the
          early 2000s, not to the 1980s games this one looks to.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The row needs its container.</strong>
          Where the list sits, whether in a pane or in something that scrolls,
          is part of the answer and 02.1 did not give one.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section>
    <details id="display">
      <summary>02.2 / One display, with the drawing outside the rows</summary>
      <p>
        The second answer stops building rows as controls. The list is one
        display set into a console pane, and a row is a line of it: a name, a
        short word at the end, a hairline beneath. Nothing is cut or raised per
        row. The display shows five lines and scrolls inside itself when the
        mount accepts more.
      </p>
      <p>
        The drawing leaves the row. A readout above the display shows one part
        at a time, larger and with no box around it: its drawing, name,
        description and figures. It shows the line under the pointer or keyboard
        focus, and otherwise the fitted part, so parts are compared by moving
        down the list before one is pressed.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Fitted</strong>
          is the lit line: lime with dark text, ending in Fitted.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Unsanctioned</strong>
          keeps the hatched band at the end of its line. The words “Unsanctioned
          · no score” are in the readout.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Forbidden</strong>
          is a red line with a red bar at its start, ending in Remove or
          Replace. It cannot be pressed; the readout names the remedy.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <PartRows022 client:load />
      <p>
        The first list uses a universal mount so that it is long enough to
        scroll. The readout following the pointer does nothing on a touch
        screen, where a part's details would appear only once it is fitted. This
        still does not show the list beside the ship in the Hangar.
      </p>
      <h3>What 02.2 showed</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The row keeps its drawing, name and some figures.</strong>
          A readout outside the list does not work, because the list is never
          used alone. It is part of the Hangar, and the large picture of a part
          is already there: on the ship and its mount.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The drawing in the row is a small helper.</strong>
          It makes the row look like this game and lets ion be told from plasma
          by colour. It does not need to be large.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The open question is the minimum.</strong>
          What a row has to hold to work, and no more, has to be settled before
          its look.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="anatomy">02.3 / What a row holds, beside the ship</h2>
    <p>
      The third answer puts the list where it is used: beside the ship, in a
      slice of the Hangar. Choose a mount under the ship and the list shows its
      parts; fitting one changes the ship. The slice starts with Playground off
      and a rail gun on the first wing mount, so the forbidden row is the first
      thing shown.
    </p>
    <p>
      A row holds four things: a small drawing with no frame, the name, one line
      of figures, and a closing word only when the part is fitted. The fitted
      row alone adds the catalog description. The rows are lines of one list in
      the parts pane, which scrolls inside itself when the list is long.
    </p>
    <p>Two marks carry the states, and they do not share a place.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A bar at the start says fitted.</strong>
        Lime with the word Fitted when the part is permitted, red with Remove or
        Replace when it is forbidden. The row is not filled, so the drawing
        keeps its colours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The hatched band at the end says unsanctioned</strong>, as kept
        from 02.1. With Playground on, one line under the list explains it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <PartRows023 client:load />
    <p>
      The strip under the slice shows the row in each state at once. The slice's
      ship, mounts and fitting rules are the game's; its setting and fitting
      live only on this page. The slice is narrower than the Hangar and leaves
      out its other panes. It has not been tried by touch.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h3>What 02.3 showed</h3>
    <p>
      Rounds 02.1 to 02.3 each designed a row for a list, borrowing from the
      tabs and other shared controls. None of them looked as if it belonged in
      the Hangar. The reason is the subject, not the styling. The subject is the
      tool the player uses to choose the part for a slot. It is one bespoke
      thing in the Hangar. It has to do its own job well and conform enough to
      not feel off. It does not have to be a reusable pattern. Its nearest
      relative is the slot on the ship drawing, which it serves, and not the
      tabs. The work is to amend the tool that is in the Hangar, not to invent
      another. How it works is known, and so is where it sits: in its pane
      beside the ship. That pane predates the design system and needs a small
      change to join it. This is the main finding of the study so far.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="tool">02.4 / The tool as it stands, beside its slot</h2>
    <p>
      This round proposes nothing. It looks at the tool in the game today, with
      a slot selected, to see what it does and what it already shares with the
      slot. Below is the Hangar itself, live. It shows and changes the ship kept
      in this browser, and it will change when the Hangar does.
    </p>
    <div class="frame">
      <iframe src="/hangar" title="The Hangar, live" loading="lazy"></iframe>
    </div>
    <p>Carried in from the earlier rounds:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A hatched band at the end of the row marks an unsanctioned part. It is
        the working answer.
      </li>
      <li>
        The row keeps a small drawing, the name and some figures. The drawing is
        a helper for telling parts apart, and the large picture is on the ship.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>What the Hangar shows today:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The tool already names its slot.</strong>
        It is headed “Selected mount” with the slot's name, and one sentence
        says whether the slot can be left empty. It does not repeat the number
        the slot carries on the ship.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fitted is said the same way in both places.</strong>
        A filled slot on the ship is a thin lime rectangle around the part. The
        fitted row is a thin lime rectangle around the part. Whether by intent
        or not, the row already reads as the slot's contents.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The same drawing appears twice.</strong>
        The part in the fitted row and the part in the slot are one drawing at
        two sizes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Selection belongs to the slot only.</strong>
        Lime corner brackets mark the slot being edited. Nothing in the tool is
        selected; its rows are fitted or not.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The two disagree on words.</strong>
        The slot is labelled Engine, Equipment or Universal; a row is tagged E,
        G or U.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Neither says anything about class today</strong>
        beyond the small caption on the row. A forbidden part looks the same on
        the ship as a permitted one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A row is tall.</strong>
        Drawing, name, two lines of figures and a description make each row
        about a fifth of the pane's height, so two or three alternatives are
        visible at once, and the launch row covers the bottom of the list.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      What this suggests, to be tested and not yet shown: keep the tool and its
      pane, and amend the rows. They may take their language from the slot, with
      the fitted part as the slot's contents and the other rows as what could go
      in. Fitted, unsanctioned and forbidden would then be said once, the same
      way on the ship and in the tool.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section><h2>03 / To be developed after 02</h2></section>
</StudyLayout>
<style>
